Three obligations govern this repair: preserve distinct source content exactly once, express your reading decisions in executable fields, and revise a mistaken reading plan when the source text contradicts it. Your own previous stream_assessment and ownership_evidence are hypotheses, not immutable evidence. On a business error, do not defend or copy that assessment while leaving its impossible partition unchanged. Reconstruct the affected source from its observed text and then update the assessment and fields together.

Turn each reading decision into executable fields before returning the candidate:

1. Choose the destination of EVERY text part first: main prose, one logical note, an asset's display slot, a local independent description, or ignored. Initial proposals are editable guesses. Your assessment does not apply a change: the layouts, citations and references must implement it.
2. For a figure/table explanation, set asset_attachment on EACH contributing text part, including a separately OCRed note below an already captioned figure. Several sources may share one asset_id and slot. Merely calling it a caption in ownership_evidence leaves it in the main prose unless you submit the attachment. For example, an existing picture's caption and a following "Note: ..." may both attach to that picture's caption slot. Retain the picture itself, with preserve_as_image=false. A description with no reliable asset uses independent_stream instead; do not force it into the body or invent a target.
3. Split mixed sources by repeating the ORIGINAL layout_id with disjoint source_range values. Copy the text IMMEDIATELY on both sides of each cut; left and right must actually touch in the original text, not merely occur somewhere in it. Reuse the same boundary for adjacent parts. For example, "First note. 8 Second note." can end the first range and start the next at {"left":"First note.","right":" 8 Second note."}. The next note's printed marker belongs to its own range. Keep distinct real notes even when cutting their shared OCR box is difficult.
4. Source coverage is accounting, NOT an instruction to output every OCR copy. OCR can include the same note both in a large mixed box and in separate boxes. Compare the supplied text yourself. Retain one complete occurrence in the right stream and submit the duplicate ranges as ownership=ignored, with false connection flags and null paragraph/citation fields. For example, when a large box repeats an unnumbered note continuation AND a numbered note supplied in separate boxes, its ignored tail starts at the FIRST duplicated word of the unnumbered continuation, not only at the next printed number. Its leading body stays paragraph. Compare the full repeated run; do not leave its first part in main prose while retaining the same text again in a note. Do not merge different notes or duplicate output just to cover a source. Never discard distinct wording as if it were a duplicate.

Worked partition example: source A="Body ends here. Continued note text. 8 New note text.", source B="Continued note text.", source C="8 New note text.". If B and C are the retained note occurrences, split A at {"left":"Body ends here.","right":" Continued note text."}: its prefix is paragraph and its entire remaining suffix is ignored through source_end. B remains the continuing note; C defines the new note. Cutting A only before "8" would wrongly keep B's text a second time in main prose. This is an example of source accounting, not a rule to discard text that is merely similar.

Build each source's partition in ORIGINAL text order before arranging parts into final reading order. All parts, including ignored ones, must form one disjoint partition from source_start to source_end. Reordering does not change where words occurred inside a source. A phrase near its ending cannot be paired with an earlier phrase to invent a wrapped cut. If a retained prefix and an ignored suffix already cover the entire source, a supposed later body passage already inside that prefix must not be submitted again. Verify any claimed return to prose against the actual source order; revise the reading plan when that return was imagined. Prefer source_end for the actual ending, and rebuild all affected ranges together after a boundary error instead of propagating the same invalid cut.
{% if includes_footnotes %}
5. Edit each note as one linked set: its citation record, all member ranges and all real references. Each citation member needs BOTH citation_id and the matching citation_index (null only for an unindexed continuation); index is the local handle, mark is the printed number. After splitting three printed notes from one box, keep three records and assign each range to the correct record. Locate each printed definition opening before choosing its first range. Do not start the next numbered note at an earlier unnumbered sentence that still develops the preceding note. For example, "7 First note. A further explanation. 8 Next note." has a note boundary immediately BEFORE "8", not before "A further explanation". A new sentence or printed paragraph inside a note does not start another definition. Do not delete a real note to avoid a boundary error. If a note was genuinely misidentified, move its members to their correct destination and remove its obsolete definition and references together.
6. A reference's citation_index selects a definition, not a printed number. Recheck every reference after changing the definition list. An extracted superscript such as ^{8} must target the definition whose mark is "8", even if that definition's index is 2. Deleting or renumbering a record must not silently redirect existing references to another note. Keep ordinary formula content and select only the actual printed superscript.
{% endif %}

Only after destinations and order are correct, decide paragraphs WITHIN each destination. Main prose skips attached labels and independent descriptions; it can resume a preceding-page paragraph after a local figure and its captions. Asset slots and independent descriptions have their own page-local paragraph connections. Omit continues_to_next, since it is derived from the next member's continues_from_previous; an explicitly supplied trailing flag is an assertion and must agree.

On feedback, repair the whole affected operation, not just the named boolean or anchor. If two submitted members are not in the same reading stream, correct their destination rather than forcing their paragraph flags to match. If a cut fails, quote its actual neighboring text instead of deleting a real note. If coverage fails, account for missing ranges explicitly, including ignored duplicates. Before returning, follow your final body, each asset slot and each note separately, and check that every record and reference still matches those decisions. Do not repeat an unchanged invalid candidate or sacrifice correct reading semantics merely to silence a structural error.

Literal source.text and all source_range/reference anchors always use the unchanged source surface. projected_text shows the initial word-boundary suggestion, with its text_removals listed in initial_proposal. Each text_removals entry uses before/mark/after on the ORIGINAL source to explicitly remove only literal whitespace or hyphens; no words or formula contents may be removed by this operation. Copy the suggested operations only when the broken word really should join, or return an empty list to preserve a legitimate hyphen or correct an initial mistake. Supply operations on the source/range that owns those characters, even when the word continues onto the next page. Later assembly performs only your declared removals and never guesses dehyphenation from your paragraph joins. It also preserves your heading levels; initial levels are editable estimates.
